ur","H6908":"to gather","H413":"to(wards)","H5104":"River","H935":"come","H163":"Ahava","H2583":"to camp","H8033":"there","H3117":"day","H995":"to understand","H5971":"people","H3548":"priest","H3878":"Levi","H3808":"not","H4672":"to find","H7971":"depart","H461":"Eliezer","H740":"Ariel","H494":"Elnathan","H3402":"Jarib","H5416":"Nathan","H4918":"Meshullam","H3114":"Joiarib","H3318":"come","H6680":"to command","H853":"[Obj.]","H5921":"upon","H112":"Iddo","H3703":"Casiphia","H4725":"place","H7760":"make","H6310":"lip","H1697":"word","H1696":"speak","H251":"male-sibling","H5411":"temple servant","H8334":"to minister","H1004":"place","H430":"God","H3027":"hand","H2896":"pleasant","H376":"man","H7922":"understanding","H4249":"Mahli","H3478":"Israel","H8274":"Sherebiah","H2811":"Hashabiah","H854":"with","H4847":"Merari","H4480":"from","H5414":"give","H8269":"ruler","H5656":"service","H3881":"Levi","H3605":"all","H5344":"to pierce","H7121":"call to","H6685":"fast","H6031":"be occupied","H6440":"before","H1245":"to seek","H1870":"conduct","H3477":"upright","H2945":"child","H7399":"property","H3588":"for","H954":"be ashamed","H7592":"to ask","H2428":"Helech","H6571":"horse","H5826":"to help","H341":"enemy","H559":"to say","H5797":"strength","H639":"anger","H5800":"forsake","H6684":"to fast","H2063":"this","H6279":"to pray","H914":"to separate","H8147":"two","H8254":"to weigh","H3701":"money","H2091":"gold","H3627":"article/utensil","H8641":"contribution","H7311":"to exalt","H3289":"to advise","H3603":"Plain (of Jericho)","H8337":"six","H3713":"bowl","H150":"dram","H505":"thousand","H5178":"bronze","H6668":"to gleam","H2532":"desire","H859":"you(m.s.)","H6944":"holiness","H3068":"LORD","H5071":"voluntariness","H8245":"to watch","H8104":"obey","H5704":"till","H3389":"Jerusalem","H3957":"chamber","H6901":"to receive","H4948":"weight","H5265":"to set out","H2320":"month","H7223":"first","H3212":"to walk","H1961":"to be","H5337":"to rescue","H3709":"palm","H693":"to ambush","H3427":"to dwell","H7243":"fourth","H4822":"Meremoth","H223":"Uriah","H499":"Eleazar","H3107":"Jozacar","H3442":"Jeshua","H5129":"Noadiah","H1131":"Binnui","H4557":"number","H3789":"to write","H6256":"time","H1931":"he/she/it","H7628":"captivity","H1473":"captivity","H7126":"come","H5930":"burnt offering","H6499":"bullock","H352":"ram","H8673":"ninety","H3532":"lamb","H7651":"seven","H6842":"male goat","H2403":"sin","H1881":"law","H323":"satrap","H6346":"governor","H5676":"beside","H5375":"raise"},"ccc":{},"cccp":{},"com":[["haydock",2,null,"haydock","Hattus. He was of the royal family, (Haydock) as the former were descendants of the two sons of Aaron. (Calmet) — The great numbers which were persuaded to return from Babylon, signify the conversions made by holy preachers. (Ven. Bede) (Worthington)"],["haydock",5,null,"haydock","Sons. Septuagint and Arabic add, “of Zathoe, Sechenias, the son of Aziel.” The Syriac supplies the name of “Gado, the son of Nehzael,” which seems to have been lost. (Calmet) — Junius translates, “of the descendants Sechenias N. son of Jahaziel.”"],["haydock",10,null,"haydock","Sons of. Septuagint supply “Baani,” whose name occurs, chap. ii. 10, and is here visibly wanting. (Calmet)"],["haydock",13,null,"haydock","Last. Coming after their brethren, chap. ii. 13."],["haydock",14,null,"haydock","Zachur. Hebrew and Septuagint, “Zabud.” (Calmet) — Two letters have been mistaken. (Haydock)"],["haydock",15,null,"haydock","Ahava. This river (ver. 21., and 31.) runs through the territory of the same name, called Hava; (4 Kings xvii. 24.) the people of which exchanged places with the Israelites. Esdras makes a circuit to prevail on some of the latter to return with him, unless he began his journey from Susa. Babylon comprises all that country. (Calmet) — None there, who were not priests, ver. 2. (Lyranus) (Menochius)"],["haydock",16,null,"haydock","Sent. Septuagint, &c., “to Eliezer,” &c., in order to give them his orders what to say to Eddo, who presided at Chasphia, as the sequel shews. — Wise men, instructors of the people, (2 Paralipomenon xxxv. 3.; Calmet) Levites. (Menochius)"],["haydock",17,null,"haydock","Chasphia. The Caspian mountains, between Media and Hyrcania. Here the Nathinites were forced to labour, under Eddo. The service of the temple would be far more eligible. (Calmet)"],["haydock",21,null,"haydock","Fast it seems for eight days, so that they departed on the 12th, ver. 31. (Menochius) — It will not suffice to leave sin, we must also do works of satisfaction. (Worthington)"],["haydock",22,null,"haydock","Forsake him. Apostates are treated with the greatest severity. (Menochius) — Esdras thought that the glory of God was at stake, and he would not shew any diffidence in Providence, or scandalize the infidels. (Calmet) — We ought not to ask princes for what we should despise. (Du Hamel)"],["haydock",26,null,"haydock","A hundred. Hebrew adds, “talents,” vessels of silver. (Haydock) — Some Rabbins suppose there were only 100, each of this weight, which is not probable. All the silver vessels, of different sizes, weighed 100 talents. There was the same quantity of golden vessels. (Calmet)"],["haydock",27,null,"haydock","Solids. Hebrew adarconim. Darics, equivalent to the golden sicle. (Calmet) (1 Paralipomenon xxix. 7.) — Best. Protestants, “of fine copper, precious as gold.” It might resemble the Corinthian brass, or aurichalcum, (Haydock) composed of gold, silver, and brass melted together, in the burning of Corinth, by L. Mummius. (Pliny, [Natural History?] xxxiv. 2.) — Yet no such Corinthian vessels have come down to us; so that the account seems fabulous; and, at any rate, the city was not taken in the days of Esdras, but in the year 608 of Rome. We cannot say whether he speaks of copper, brass, &c. (Calmet)"],["haydock",35,null,"haydock","Israel. Some of the different tribes certainly returned, chap. vi. 17."],["haydock",36,null,"haydock","Lords, (satrapis.) 3 Esdras, “to the royal officers and governors of Cœlosyria and Phenicia; and they glorified,” &c. — Furthered, by their assistance (Haydock) and praise. They formed their sentiments, as is usual, by those of the king. (Calmet)"],["challoner",21,null,"challoner","And I proclaimed a fast.... It is not enough to part from Babylon, that is, figuratively from sin, but we must also do works of penance; and therefore Esdras here proclaimed an extraordinary fast to those that were come from captivity. This shews that fasting was commanded and practised from the earliest times."]],"au":{"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"],"challoner":["Richard Challoner",0,0,1781,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/03566a.htm"]}}